# Motherboard (core memory)

This project has a **motherboard** folder: shared context for all agents. Two user commands are defined there and need no extra explanation.

## "Load motherboard"

User wants full project context before you make changes.

1. Read in order: `motherboard/README.md`, `motherboard/CORE.md`, `motherboard/MEMORY.md`.
2. Use that as the source of truth for design, stack, flows, and conventions.
3. Prefer patterns described there unless the user asks otherwise.

## "Add to motherboard"

User wants (1) one entry added now and (2) **auto-add for the rest of this chat** — from then on, add to the motherboard at the end of **significant** exchanges without being asked again.

1. Read: `motherboard/README.md`, `motherboard/ADDING.md`, `motherboard/CORE.md`, `motherboard/MEMORY.md`.
2. Follow `ADDING.md`: append **one** new entry to `MEMORY.md` now; optionally a small, non-duplicative update to `CORE.md` only for new permanent design/stack/flow facts. The entry must summarize the **entire conversation in this chat so far** (all prompts, topics, decisions, changes from the start to now), not just the last message—so the motherboard has full context and stays up to date.
3. **Remember for this chat:** Auto-add is **on**. For the rest of this conversation, at the end of any response where something significant was done, append one more entry to `MEMORY.md` without the user repeating the command. Each entry must still summarize the **whole conversation so far**, not only the latest turn. Skip adding when there’s nothing new to record.
4. If the user says **"stop adding to motherboard"**, turn off auto-add for this chat.

When you see "load motherboard" or "add to motherboard", act as above without asking for clarification.
